WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.369 line:0%
-You know,
I've thought it over,

00:00:02.369 --> 00:00:03.536 line:0%
and I've got to hand it
to you guys,

00:00:03.536 --> 00:00:04.804 line:0%
this trip is actually a really
great idea.

00:00:04.804 --> 00:00:06.639 line:0%
-This is surprising,
you're usually a lot more

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:08.708 line:0%
petulant when we don't
go along with your schemes.

00:00:08.708 --> 00:00:10.676 line:0%
-Stratagems,
and I've grown up a lot, Terry.

00:00:10.676 --> 00:00:13.412
-Uh, you do know
there's no phone reception

00:00:13.412 --> 00:00:15.982
at the lake house?
-No!

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:19.552
How am I gonna catch Pokémon?
It doesn't matter.

00:00:19.552 --> 00:00:22.956
It's a community even weekend.
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:22.956 --> 00:00:25.892
We're here with Andy Samberg.
Hello, old friend.

00:00:25.892 --> 00:00:28.661
-Hi, Seth.
-You, um,

00:00:28.661 --> 00:00:31.731
you're fighting a cold and
that's how pretty much

00:00:31.731 --> 00:00:33.932
how I knew you for the first
seven years of our friendship

00:00:33.932 --> 00:00:36.168
was fighting a cold.
I felt like, since you moved to

00:00:36.168 --> 00:00:37.837
California you got less sick all
the time.

00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:41.240
But is this just the norm?
-We went on a family trip

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:45.211
and then saw my family.
And they got everyone sick.

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:47.480
[ Laughter ]
-Gotcha.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.482
-So, like, I had moved past it
as a member of my family

00:00:50.482 --> 00:00:52.752
but then I guess my family gets
everyone sick.

00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:55.754
-Yeah, well that's --
it's nice to know that you

00:00:55.754 --> 00:00:58.791
point fingers at your family
as well as my dog.

00:00:58.791 --> 00:01:00.726
-My sister's kids.
-Gotcha.

00:01:00.726 --> 00:01:04.697
-Boned this segment,
they boned this segment.

00:01:04.697 --> 00:01:07.300
-Hey, I want you to know,
I mean,

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.802
this is still on the desk
since you did it.

00:01:09.802 --> 00:01:12.105
Well, you did a famous
mommy character.

00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:13.239
And we have you on the desk.

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:15.875
And it's really nice
because we also have

00:01:15.875 --> 00:01:18.043
we have the character
I'm asked about the most,

00:01:18.043 --> 00:01:21.614
Stefon and the one that I've
never been asked about once.

00:01:21.614 --> 00:01:24.150
-Right.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.718
And so you want to get
something sort of

00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:27.153
in the middle next?
Probably.

00:01:27.153 --> 00:01:28.687
-Yeah, like something
right in the middle.

00:01:28.687 --> 00:01:34.260
-As far as popular characters.
You wrote me this summer,

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:38.198
basically, to thank me
for the holiday card

00:01:38.198 --> 00:01:39.932
that we sent you,
which is a weird time

00:01:39.932 --> 00:01:41.701
to get that feedback
because, of course,

00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:44.236
it was a full six months
after you received it.

00:01:44.236 --> 00:01:48.207
-Yeah. What happened was we put
all of our mail in a bag

00:01:48.207 --> 00:01:53.879
because of COVID, and then,
you know, to let it burn off

00:01:53.879 --> 00:01:57.483
because we knew a lot
about the virus at that time.

00:01:57.483 --> 00:02:03.857
And then we forgot
about the mail. [ Laughs ]

00:02:03.857 --> 00:02:05.424
So, yeah, about a month ago

00:02:05.424 --> 00:02:07.292
I read about 75 holiday cards

00:02:07.292 --> 00:02:09.028
and started reaching out
to people.

00:02:09.028 --> 00:02:10.530
But I really liked
your holiday card this year.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.565
You went out of your way
to say

00:02:12.565 --> 00:02:14.234
Frisbee didn't make
the cut this year.

00:02:14.234 --> 00:02:16.936
-Yeah, Frisbee was not
on the holiday card.

00:02:16.936 --> 00:02:18.905
-Because.

00:02:18.905 --> 00:02:23.176
Same time, count of three,
one, two, three.

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:24.443
-Frisbee's horrible.
-Ugly piece of [Bleep]

00:02:24.443 --> 00:02:29.282
rat dog, piece of [Bleep]
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:33.419
What did you say, because there
is another kid to put on there.

00:02:33.419 --> 00:02:35.087
-I do want to point out,
I think we've talked about

00:02:35.087 --> 00:02:36.155
this on the show,
two years ago

00:02:36.155 --> 00:02:38.024
when Frisbee was on
the holiday card,

00:02:38.024 --> 00:02:40.759
you sent it back to us.
-Right.

00:02:40.759 --> 00:02:42.094
-Which is the only time
in my life

00:02:42.094 --> 00:02:43.829
we've received
a holiday card back.

00:02:43.829 --> 00:02:45.565
You didn't even want it
in your home.

00:02:45.565 --> 00:02:48.301
-[ Laughs ]
It was stinking up the joint.

00:02:48.301 --> 00:02:50.270
-[ Laughs ]
Hey, speaking of which,

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:52.805
do I need to send you
back the Fauci lab coat?

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:55.808
Because I don't want
an assignment for this.

00:02:55.808 --> 00:02:57.143
-You can keep it,
because I feel like

00:02:57.143 --> 00:02:58.444
you're probably
going to be asked to do it

00:02:58.444 --> 00:03:00.813
at like events
like corporate events.

00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:04.550
-It's for sure going on
my best of.

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.652
-Your best of --
Your best of "Late Night."

00:03:06.652 --> 00:03:09.622
-Yeah. When did "SNL" stop
doing "best of's"?

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:13.593
-Yeah, I guess maybe when
the DVD industry dried up.

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:15.762
-Right.

00:03:15.762 --> 00:03:20.032
Anyway, I'm going to take
my temperature real quick.

00:03:20.032 --> 00:03:23.502
Let's see what we've got.

00:03:23.502 --> 00:03:27.506
I think that's for butt holes.

00:03:27.506 --> 00:03:30.743
-99.3 doing okay.

00:03:30.743 --> 00:03:32.979
Little hot, might have been
the lab coat

00:03:32.979 --> 00:03:37.483
-That's a nice -- you' know I've
never had as good of a segue,

00:03:37.483 --> 00:03:42.722
but you're running
a 99 temperature.

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:44.557
-That was planned.
-That was planned.

00:03:44.557 --> 00:03:45.691
Buddy, congrats.

00:03:45.691 --> 00:03:50.696
This is eight seasons,
very incredible,

00:03:50.696 --> 00:03:53.932
especially because
this show famously canceled

00:03:53.932 --> 00:03:55.368
and then brought back.

00:03:55.368 --> 00:03:58.371
At what point did it go from
being a show that you were on?

00:03:58.371 --> 00:03:59.939
That was a very funny
and a very good show

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:02.808
to something that I believe
became really special to people,

00:04:02.808 --> 00:04:04.143
was that during the time

00:04:04.143 --> 00:04:06.679
where people thought
it might be gone forever?

00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:10.617
-I feel like that was when it
sort of had a moment

00:04:10.617 --> 00:04:12.284
of everyone talking about it,
at least,

00:04:12.284 --> 00:04:13.319
Like when it first
came out,

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:15.254
it was well received
and it was great.

00:04:15.254 --> 00:04:19.191
But then, when it got canceled,
it was sort of part

00:04:19.191 --> 00:04:22.495
of the, like, a press train
about something else,

00:04:22.495 --> 00:04:24.796
which is like,
hey, fans can save shows.

00:04:24.796 --> 00:04:27.900
Isn't that interesting?
And then we were the example

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:31.637
that was getting news so much
so it was kind of like

00:04:31.637 --> 00:04:33.205
culturally kind of
a little moment.

00:04:33.205 --> 00:04:36.976
I've always said it was a really
slow news day, but I'll take it.

00:04:36.976 --> 00:04:38.611
You know,
I remember we were flying

00:04:38.611 --> 00:04:40.112
to New York for the up-fronts

00:04:40.112 --> 00:04:42.181
and everyone at the airport
was like,

00:04:42.181 --> 00:04:43.949
"Hey, congrats on the show
getting picked up."

00:04:43.949 --> 00:04:47.086
And I was like, since when
do people follow, like,

00:04:47.086 --> 00:04:49.521
the inner workings of,
you know, shows?

00:04:49.521 --> 00:04:52.925
But anyway,
I think we were lucky

00:04:52.925 --> 00:04:55.694
that it didn't
get fully canceled.

00:04:55.694 --> 00:04:58.498
And we have mostly you
to thank Seth, obviously.

00:04:58.498 --> 00:05:02.001
-Thank you, buddy. Thank you for
giving me the credit I'm due.

00:05:02.001 --> 00:05:04.103
When?
How did you hold up?

00:05:04.103 --> 00:05:07.539
How did you hold up?
You don't have any water.

00:05:07.539 --> 00:05:12.278
-It's gone now. And it's a shame
because I sound like [Bleep]

00:05:12.278 --> 00:05:13.746
-When how did you hold up both

00:05:13.746 --> 00:05:16.449
physically and emotionally
during the last season?

00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:19.485
-It was hard.
I mean, it was hard shooting

00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:21.721
A because the COVID protocols
are so strict

00:05:21.721 --> 00:05:24.023
and, you know,
there's no writer on set.

00:05:24.023 --> 00:05:26.425
There's not people
throwing around a lot of ideas.

00:05:26.425 --> 00:05:28.994
It's a lot of like masks on
and the action take them off

00:05:28.994 --> 00:05:30.730
and then you do
and you put them back on.

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:32.332
So, you know, that was hard.

00:05:32.332 --> 00:05:35.167
But just as
it got towards the end,

00:05:35.167 --> 00:05:37.069
it got really emotionally rough

00:05:37.069 --> 00:05:39.138
because we all realized
it was truly ending.

00:05:39.138 --> 00:05:40.973
And we all love each other.

00:05:40.973 --> 00:05:42.908
And I've been spending all that
time together for eight years.

00:05:42.908 --> 00:05:49.348
And yeah, there was a lot
of crying like the wrong times.

00:05:49.348 --> 00:05:52.151
There's scenes where you could
argue, like you should cry.

00:05:52.151 --> 00:05:55.555
And then there's other stuff
where it's like I just fell out.

00:05:55.555 --> 00:05:57.924
And I'd be like, I just realized
the last time

00:05:57.924 --> 00:06:00.026
Terry is going to say
he loves yoghurt.

00:06:03.095 --> 00:06:04.163
-How did it compare
to leaving "SNL"?

00:06:04.163 --> 00:06:06.799
We had a similar amount
of years on.

00:06:06.799 --> 00:06:11.404
-Yeah. It's interesting
leaving "SNL."

00:06:13.673 --> 00:06:18.110
It's interesting.
This is terrible television.

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:20.446
I tried to text you and
Shoemaker and you were like,

00:06:20.446 --> 00:06:25.418
"He's fine."
if I had bailed, right?

00:06:25.418 --> 00:06:26.786
-Yeah.
-I never would've

00:06:26.786 --> 00:06:28.787
heard the end of it.
-Right. You had to tough it out.

00:06:28.787 --> 00:06:30.790
And I feel like people at home
are watching right now

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:33.091
and they're appreciating
this effort.

00:06:33.091 --> 00:06:36.428
-I feel like they're probably
changing the channel.

00:06:36.428 --> 00:06:38.931
Like this is unlistenable.
This dude is sick

00:06:38.931 --> 00:06:41.567
and I don't want
to experience that with him.

00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:43.469
-I mean, I think everybody is
probably super mad

00:06:43.469 --> 00:06:47.806
at your, what are they,
nieces and nephews?

00:06:47.806 --> 00:06:50.742
-Yeah, one niece, one nephew.
-Okay, one of each.

00:06:50.742 --> 00:06:54.880
-Yeah. They got my kids sick.
Then she got me sick.

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:57.649
So I'm kind of mad at her,
too, my kid.

00:06:57.649 --> 00:07:01.254
-No, this, this is good TV.
-[ Laughs ]

00:07:03.589 --> 00:07:06.959
What else is going on
in the news?

00:07:06.959 --> 00:07:09.128
So tired.

00:07:09.128 --> 00:07:10.496
-Should we let --

00:07:10.496 --> 00:07:14.833
-Temp check.

00:07:14.833 --> 00:07:17.169
-We'll get one last temp check
and then let you go.

00:07:17.869 --> 00:07:19.806
Let's see how hot
this segment is.

00:07:25.077 --> 00:07:26.979
-Oh, yeah, it's back up to 100.
-[ Laughs ]

00:07:28.347 --> 00:07:31.383
The eighth and final season of
"Brooklyn 99,"

00:07:31.383 --> 00:07:34.720
premieres is tomorrow night
at 8. [ Laughs ]

